(Think of a toll booth. The booth collects the toll from you, but the money is not the booth's. It goes to the) Tj
T* (government. GST is the same. When Laavin sends a Rs. 1,00,000 invoice with 18 percent GST, the) Tj
T* (client pays Rs. 1,18,000. Rs. 1,00,000 is Laavin's income. The Rs. 18,000 is the government's, and) Tj
T* (Laavin holds it until it files its GST return and pays.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
14.9499999999999993 TL
0.141 0.275 0.227 rg
56.6929133858267775 562.932519685039324 Td
(Input GST: the tax you paid when buying) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.165 0.173 0.161 rg
56.6929133858267775 539.8301574803149379 Td
(When Laavin buys materials, the supplier also charges GST. That tax was paid on the way in, so Laavin) Tj
T* (can set it off against the GST it collected. Example: collected Rs. 18,000 on sales, paid Rs. 3,600 on) Tj
T* (purchases. Laavin only has to pay the government the difference: Rs. 14,400.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
14.9499999999999993 TL
0.141 0.275 0.227 rg
56.6929133858267775 479.8632283464566513 Td
(CGST, SGST and IGST: only a split) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.165 0.173 0.161 rg
56.6929133858267775 456.7608661417322082 Td
(The GST rate is the same. Only the name changes by place. If you and the client are in the same state,) Tj
T* (the tax is split in two equal halves, CGST \(centre\) and SGST \(state\). If you are in different states, the) Tj
T* (whole of it is called IGST. Either way, 18 percent is 18 percent. LIS looks at the first two digits of the) Tj
T* (GST numbers to decide.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 13 Tf
14.9499999999999993 TL
0.141 0.275 0.227 rg
56.6929133858267775 382.8049606299211405 Td
(TDS: tax paid for you in advance) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 10.5 Tf
12.0749999999999993 TL
0.165 0.173 0.161 rg
56.6929133858267775 359.7025984251967543 Td
(Some clients are required to keep back a small part of your bill \(say 2 percent\) and deposit it with the) Tj
T* (income tax department in your name. You do not receive that money, but it is not lost: it counts as tax) Tj
T* (you have already paid, and you claim it back when you file your income tax. So LIS records it as "TDS) Tj
T* (receivable", a credit you hold. The same thing works the other way when Laavin pays its own suppliers:) Tj
T* (Laavin keeps back a part and deposits it for them.) Tj
ET
